  "narrative": {
    "essence": "France enters 2026 as the co-favorite and arguably the most complete squad in the world — the rare team that can win ugly or win beautifully. The only question is whether Mbappé can finally carry his nation across the finish line at a tournament, not just to it.",
    "footballIdentity": "France's football story is inseparable from its immigration story. Most of this squad grew up in the banlieues of Paris, Lyon, and Marseille — products of a system that funnels talent from the suburbs into elite academies. When France wins, the country celebrates. When they lose, it debates what it means to be French. That weight follows them everywhere.",
    "theMoment": "France enters 2026 in a politically fractured moment. Macron's snap election gamble in 2024 left him weakened, with a hung parliament and no clear governing majority. Growth is sluggish, debt is elevated, and social cohesion is strained. A World Cup run would be more than sport — it would be a rare moment of national unity in a country that badly needs one. The 1998 win briefly transcended all of it. France is chasing that again.",
    "momentType": "political"
  },
